{
 "id": "l17",
 "label": "17",
 "title": "Pseudo verbs: dápat, gustó and others",
 "summary": "Must, can, need, like and don't like: dápat, maáarì, puwéde, kailángan, ibig, gustó and áyaw",
 "minutes": 35,
 "goals": [
  "Know the meanings of the pseudo verbs dápat, maáarì, puwéde, kailángan, ibig, gustó and áyaw",
  "Use only the infinitive form of the main verb after a pseudo verb",
  "Put ko or mo between ibig/gustó and the main verb",
  "Choose a nominative pronoun (akó, siyá) or a possessive pronoun (niyá) for the other pseudo verbs",
  "Know when a ligature is needed"
 ],
 "sections": [
  {
   "title": "What pseudo verbs do",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "type": "p",
     "t": "**Pseudo verbs** go with a main (principal) verb. They shift its meaning to show that an action **should** or **should not** have been done, or **can** or **cannot** be carried out, and so on. The English helpers *must, can, need to* and *like to* do a similar job."
    },
    {
     "type": "table",
     "head": [
      "Pseudo verb",
      "Meaning"
     ],
     "rows": [
      [
       "[[dápat|pred]]",
       "must, should, would, has/have to"
      ],
      [
       "[[maáarì|pred]] or [[puwéde|pred]]",
       "can, may, could"
      ],
      [
       "[[kailángan|pred]]",
       "needs to, is necessary to"
      ],
      [
       "[[ibig|pred]] or [[gustó|pred]]",
       "likes, desires, wants"
      ],
      [
       "[[áyaw|pred]]",
       "does not like"
      ]
     ],
